{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"RUNTIME VERIFICATION COMPLETE — ALL THREE ARMS GREEN on a live run, which is the one thing the\nimplementation could not evidence for itself.\n\nRUN 30846201728, head d4095763e (VERIFIED to contain the [llm] model fix before reading any result):\n    success   external LLM (live anthropic)\n    success   external LLM (live openai)\n    success   external LLM (live bedrock)\n    success   external integration (live services)\n    success   langfuse tracing round-trip (ephemeral self-hosted)\nSo the matrix runs one arm per credentialed provider, all three execute against real providers, and all\nthree pass. AC1/AC2 are now demonstrated at runtime rather than only statically.\n\nTHE ROUTE HERE MATTERED, AND THE FIRST ATTEMPT WAS A FALSE NEGATIVE WORTH RECORDING.\nRun 30836378745 (pre-fix) failed the bedrock and openai arms. Run 30843695520 failed them IDENTICALLY —\nand I nearly read that as the fix not working. It was not: that runs head was f2b2d0b41, which I\nverified does NOT contain the fix. Gerrit merged at 18:58:32 and the dispatch was 18:58:47, before the\nGitHub MIRROR replicated, so Actions checked out main as GitHub knew it — pre-fix code. The lesson is\nconcrete: after a Gerrit submit, a CI dispatch must wait for mirror replication, and an arm result must\nnever be read without first asserting the runs head SHA carries the change. Both watchers now do that.\n\nWHAT THE PRE-FIX FAILURES PROVED, which is the storys actual point: the arms failed LOUDLY rather than\nskipping to green. The bedrock arm reported `[llm-live-canary] collected=14 executed=14 skipped=0` with\n11 tests making real Bedrock calls and passing. A vacuous green was impossible — which is exactly the\nfailure mode AC4 exists to prevent, demonstrated by a real red rather than argued.\n\nTHE DEFECT THE LIVE RUN FOUND, now fixed (Gerrit 1308): the overlays set only [llm.model_classes], but\ncfg.model is a SECOND resolution path the class table cannot reach — config.py:575 falls back to\nDEFAULT_MODEL, the bare literal \"claude-opus-4-8\", which infers provider anthropic. Three tests read\ncfg.model rather than naming a class and so called direct Anthropic on EVERY arm, failing with \"Could not\nresolve authentication method\" because non-Anthropic arms deliberately blank ANTHROPIC_API_KEY.\n\nAND THIS STORYS OWN GUARD HAD THE SAME BLIND SPOT — worth recording as a design lesson, not just a bug:\n`test_every_model_class_resolves_to_the_declared_provider` PASSED on all three arms while those arms\ncalled Anthropic, because it only checks the three CLASS names and never cfg.model. Its docstring claims\nto catch \"a partial overlay would leave some ops on the default provider, which is the ambient default\nthis story removes\" — so the assertion was over the WRONG SURFACE for the storys stated goal. Added\n`test_the_ambient_default_model_also_resolves_to_the_declared_provider`, which asserts\nLLMConfig.from_env().model is provider-qualified.\nWORSE, A TEST WAS ENFORCING THE BUG: `test_each_overlay_sets_only_the_model_classes_table` asserted\nset(data[\"llm\"]) == {\"model_classes\"}, so the fix could not land without that test failing\n(`Extra items in the left set: model`). Its module docstring encoded the same assumption. It now pins\nBOTH selection keys and still forbids unrelated ones, which was its real intent. A test that pins the\nwrong surface is worse than no test, because it certifies the gap.\n\nALSO FIXED: review_skeleton.yamls model_ladder was three literal claude-* ids, which pinned an Anthropic\nladder into any providers run; now [trivial, standard, frontier]. No bare ladder id remains under\nworkflow/. test_review_kernel_rules_live needed NO change — its docstring already claimed the overlay\nrepoints it, which was false before the fix and is true after.\n\nVERIFIED TOKEN-FREE that the overlay now repoints the ambient default per arm:\n    anthropic -> cfg.model=anthropic:claude-opus-4-8\n    bedrock   -> cfg.model=bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-opus-4-8\n    openai    -> cfg.model=openai:gpt-5.4\n\nOPERATOR ACTIONS THAT UNBLOCKED THIS, recorded so the sequence is reconstructable: the OpenAI arm was\npreviously 429 insufficient_quota (the key AUTHENTICATED — 429 not 401 — and OpenAI echoed model_name\nback, so the ids were always real and entitled); a funded key was set as the OPENAI_API_KEY repository\nsecret. The IAM role rebar-external-ci-bedrock was created with trust scoped to\nrepository=navapbc/rebar and sub EXACTLY repo:navapbc/rebar:ref:refs/heads/main (StringEquals, no glob),\npermissions limited to bedrock:InvokeModel* on anthropic.claude-*, no managed policies. Role assumption\nand region resolution both confirmed in-run.\n\nSTILL TO CONFIRM FROM THE LOGS once the run fully completes (the jira DC job is still going and GitHub\nserves no logs until then): each arms `[llm-live-canary] executed=N` figure, so a green is recorded as\ngenuinely-executed rather than inferred from a job conclusion."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785786255936614001,"uuid":"6c095637-38ee-4c6a-a2ca-0383df23860c"}